Chapter 16 – Part 1
It was late by the time Curtis got home.
He locked the door behind him, dropped the photo of Justice on the side table with more care than he gave most things, and only then pulled out his phone. The apartment was quiet. The city noise stayed outside where it belonged.
He opened the chat room.
Hanma was still there in the member list, exactly where it had been since the night he first introduced himself and then mostly lurked. He scrolled.
A lot had happened while he was busy with rooftops, underpasses, stab wounds, and information brokers.

Earlier threads, still visible:
TarouTanaka had announced moving to Ikebukuro that same day.
Kanra had immediately pushed for a meetup and brought up the urban legend of the Black Rider.
TarouTanaka claimed he’d seen the Rider on his first day in the city.
Setton replied that she’d been out there too, and for a moment the chat had turned almost wistful — the idea that two of them might have passed on the street without knowing it.
Later:
They talked about suicide groups.
People going missing around the city.
Setton and Kanra both warning TarouTanaka to stay away from a gang called the Dollars.
Then the Black Rider again — this time the headless version of the rumor.
Setton called it ridiculous.
TarouTanaka sounded intrigued.
Kanra stated flatly that the Black Rider was not human.
A new member had appeared in one of the more recent bursts, posted a string of disjointed words, and vanished.
Kanra later announced the troll had been banned, then casually shared rumors about Yellow Scarves members being attacked by the city’s serial slasher — while neatly ignoring TarouTanaka’s follow-up questions.

Curtis sat on the edge of the couch, long black braids falling forward as he scrolled back through it all with half-lidded eyes.
Same city. Same shadows. Different window into them.
He watched the cursor blink in the input field for a few seconds, thumb hovering.
Then, for the first time since introducing himself, Hanma decided to join the conversation properly.
Chat Room
Hanma:
Been lurking since I joined. Figured I should actually say something before people decide I’m a ghost account.
Kanra:
Oh? The mysterious new one finally speaks. Welcome to the land of the living, Hanma ♪
TarouTanaka:
Hi! I’m TarouTanaka. I only moved to Ikebukuro recently, so I’m still figuring the city out. Nice to meet you.
Setton:
Setton.
I work nights a lot, so I’m around at odd hours.
Kanra:
And I’m Kanra, obviously. Local information enthusiast, professional menace, and the only one here with proper conversational standards.
Hanma:
Hanma.
Relatively new to the city myself. Still learning which rumors are noise and which ones have teeth.
TarouTanaka:
Then we’re in the same boat. I feel like every day I hear something new that sounds fake until someone else confirms it.
Setton:
Ikebukuro does that.
Best not to believe everything, but also don’t ignore the things that keep coming back up.
Kanra:
Listen to Setton. She has the soul of a cautious older sibling.
So, Hanma — what brings a night owl like you into our humble little corner of the internet? Boredom? Curiosity? Or are you another one chasing urban legends already?
Hanma:
Mostly curiosity.
And the city doesn’t really sleep, so neither do I.
TarouTanaka:
That makes three of us then.
Welcome, Hanma.
Curtis was still looking at the chat room when his phone vibrated with a different notification.
A private message.
Unknown number — no.
The one he’d saved earlier under the streetlights.
Celty:
Are you home.
He stared at the message for a second, then typed back.
Curtis:
Yeah. Just got in a while ago.
You?
Celty:
Home.
In my room.
A pause. The typing indicator appeared, vanished, then returned.
Celty:
I almost didn’t message you.
Then I did anyway.
Curtis:
I’m glad you did.
Another short silence stretched between the two screens.
Celty:
The conversation earlier.
On the street.
I keep thinking about it.
Curtis:
Which part.
Celty:
Most of it.
You being able to hear me.
What you said about Shinra.
What you said about home.
A longer pause.
Celty:
I don’t usually keep talking to people after nights like that.
Most of the time I put the helmet back on and let the city swallow it.
Curtis:
You don’t have to swallow it with me.
If you want to talk, talk.
If you want quiet, that’s fine too.
On the other end of the city, in the dark of her room, Celty looked at that last message longer than she meant to.
Celty:
…Quiet is harder than it should be right now.
Curtis:
Then don’t force it.
I’m here.
The chat room continued scrolling faintly in the background on his other tab, full of handles and rumors and carefully half-true versions of the city.
This conversation was quieter.
And somehow heavier.
Curtis set the phone face-down on the couch cushion and reached for the remote. The television flickered on, filling the apartment with low blue light and the muted noise of some late-night program he wasn’t really watching.
He leaned back, long black braids spilling over the cushion, and let out a quiet sigh toward the empty room.
“…Justice, if you were here, would you already have a girlfriend?” A short, dry breath of amusement left him. “Heheh. Probably. You and your harem relationships.”
The joke landed soft in the quiet. For a moment it almost felt like the old forest nights — talking into the dark just to keep the shape of someone from thinning too much. Then the apartment settled back into being only his.

Across the city, Celty was still typing.
Celty:
Are you still up.
Celty:
You went quiet.
Celty:
If you fell asleep, just… ignore this later.
She stared at the screen longer than she wanted to, yellow helmet resting on the desk beside her, the dark of the room pressing in around the glow of the PDA. When no reply came, she typed one more message anyway, slower this time.
Celty:
I keep almost putting the phone down.
Then I pick it back up.
Celty:
This is your fault.
On Curtis’s couch, the phone vibrated once against the cushion. Then again. Then again.
He didn’t pick it up right away.
The television kept murmuring.
Somewhere in the space between the screen light and the dark, the absence of Justice and the presence of a new name on his phone sat side by side without resolving.
Curtis finally reached over and picked up the phone.
The screen lit his face in the dark of the apartment.
Celty’s unanswered messages sat at the top.
Then a new notification slid in.
Anri:
Are you still awake?
I made it home safely.
…Thank you again for today.
Curtis barely had time to process it before another one arrived right under it.
Rio:
Hey. You get home okay?
I couldn’t sleep.
Just checking.
His face went pale.
“…Uh oh.”

Somewhere beyond the reach of the living, in whatever thin place memory and spirit still overlapped, Justice lost it.
Oh, you absolute disaster.
The laughter came bright and helpless, the same way it always had when Curtis walked straight into trouble with both eyes open and still somehow managed to look surprised by the consequences.
One night. One night of being decent to people, and now you’ve got the headless rider, the quiet sword girl, and the rooftop girl all sitting in your phone like unread consequences.
More laughter, warmer this time, edged with the old fond exasperation only a brother could carry.
I leave you alone for five minutes in a new city and you start collecting hearts like it’s a side quest. Classic. Absolutely classic.
In the apartment, Curtis sat frozen on the couch, phone in hand, the television murmuring uselessly in the background while three different threads of the night waited for him to answer.
And somewhere Justice kept laughing, because of course this was how it began.
Curtis tilted his head back against the couch and stared at the ceiling like it might offer an exit strategy.
“…Fuck all kinds of duck.”
The phone vibrated again in his hand.
Celty:
You’re still not answering.
If you’re busy, just say so.
Another buzz.
Anri:
You don’t have to reply if you’re already asleep.
I just wanted you to know I got home.
Buzz.
Rio:
Okay that sounded more serious than I meant.
I’m fine.
You can ignore this.
…Unless you want to talk.
Curtis slowly brought the phone back up in front of his face. The screen was a quiet disaster of unanswered care, three different people reaching toward him from three different corners of the same night.
From that thin place just past living memory, Justice’s laughter came back twice as hard.
Oh, you’re cooked.
Absolutely cooked.
Look at you. One forest ghost short of adult supervision and already drowning in unread affection.
Curtis dragged a hand down his face.
The phone vibrated again.
He didn’t even look at which name it was this time.
