Chapter 16 – Part 1 (continued)
Curtis sat forward on the couch, exhaled once, and started typing.

To Celty:
Sorry. Phone was face-down.
I’m here.
Not busy. Just needed a minute.
You can keep talking if you want.

To Anri:
Good. I’m glad you made it home.
I’m still up.
You don’t have to thank me so much… but I don’t mind hearing it either.
Rest well, Anri-chan.

To Rio:
I’m home.
You don’t have to apologize for checking.
If you can’t sleep, you can text.
I’m here.

He sent the last one, locked the screen, and let the phone rest against his knee.
The apartment was quiet again. The television kept murmuring to itself. Outside, Ikebukuro continued its late-night rhythm like nothing in the city had shifted.
Curtis leaned back into the couch, long black braids spilling over the cushion, and stared at the dark ceiling with the faint, resigned expression of a man who had just answered three different doors without fully admitting he’d opened any of them.
The reply came faster than he expected.
Celty:
Okay.
Good.
A short pause. Then another message.
Celty:
I wasn’t going to keep messaging if you didn’t answer.
That was the plan.
Then I kept doing it anyway.
Celty:
Shinra has company over.
Someone from his past.
I’m in my room.
Another beat.
Celty:
It feels strange to tell you that.
I don’t usually report where I am to people.
Celty:
But you said I could keep talking.
So I am.
On the couch, Curtis watched the messages arrive one after another, the glow of the screen cutting clean through the dark of the apartment. The television continued its low noise in the background, irrelevant now.
Outside, the city kept moving.
Inside, the Black Rider was still choosing his name over silence.
Curtis stared at the screen for a second, then typed back with the same dry delivery he used in person.
Curtis:
Greaaat.
Is he also just as quirky.
If Shinra tells him about me, that’ll be two people who probably want to cut me open.
The reply took a moment.
Celty:
It’s a woman.
A short pause.
Celty:
Old classmate.
Dr. Mei.
They were close before.
Apparently they still are.
Another message followed more slowly.
Celty:
She’s in the other room with him right now.
Laughing.
I can hear it through the wall.
Celty:
I don’t know why I’m telling you that either.
On the couch, Curtis’s expression flattened slightly as he read the correction. The joke about being cut open lost some of its humor under the quieter implication sitting underneath her words.
Curtis didn’t reply right away.
He sat there on the couch with the phone still lit in his hand, the television murmuring uselessly in the background, and let the information settle.
A woman.
Old classmate.
Close before.
Still close enough to be in the apartment late, laughing through the wall while Celty sat alone in her room texting someone she had only really spoken to properly that same night.
His mismatched eyes narrowed slightly at the screen.
He wasn’t shocked. People were people. Shinra having history outside the relationship didn’t surprise him. What landed harder was the shape of it from Celty’s side — the way she reported it so carefully, like she wasn’t sure whether she was allowed to notice, or whether noticing made her ungrateful for the home she still returned to.
Curtis leaned back, long black braids shifting against the cushion, and stared at the dark ceiling for a moment.
So that’s the house she calls stable.
The earlier street conversation came back in pieces: the experiments, the years of dependence, the way she had flinched when he called it manipulation. Now there was laughter through the wall and an old classmate with a title and shared history, while the headless woman who lived there was in her room talking to a near-stranger just to feel heard.
His thumb hovered over the keyboard.
He didn’t type yet.
Part of him wanted to say something sharp.
Part of him wanted to say nothing at all.
The larger part of him recognized the familiar danger of opening his mouth when someone was already sitting in a soft, unprotected place.
So he stayed quiet a little longer, phone heavy in his hand, and let the unease work itself out before he decided what version of honesty she actually needed from him.
Curtis kept staring at the dark ceiling, phone resting against his chest as the thoughts ordered themselves more carefully.
So he has a woman over from his past.
Celty shouldn’t worry. She’s with him. They could just be friends.
The easy version of the story sat there first, neat and reasonable. People kept old classmates. People laughed. Proximity didn’t automatically mean betrayal.
Then the rest of his mind, the part trained by forests and liars and the cost of assuming too fast, pushed back.
I can’t really say.
I haven’t seen her.
I don’t know the body language, and I can’t hear what’s going on.
Therefore I cannot make an observation based off Celty’s claim alone.
He exhaled slowly through his nose.
That was the problem. From here, all he had was a report through a phone screen: an old classmate, shared history, laughter through a wall, and Celty alone in her room choosing his name over the noise in the rest of the apartment. It was enough to feel the imbalance. It was not enough to declare the shape of it.
Hmmm.
Curtis turned his head slightly toward the muted television light, mismatched eyes half-lidded.
Jumping in with certainty would make him no better than the people who built whole stories out of fragments. He had already called Celty’s relationship manipulative once tonight. Doing it again from a distance, with even less evidence, would just be noise.
So he held the conclusion back.
Not because he trusted Shinra.
Because he refused to invent what he could not yet verify.
